      "response": "{\"ops\":[{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i1\",\"find\":\"\",\"replace\":\"We aim to follow the principle of least privilege. For AWS and Kubernetes cluster access, we provide a set of IAM and Kubernetes roles, each granting the permissions engineers need for routine work. A separate, more tightly gated set of roles grants permissions for destructive or sensitive actions.\"},{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i2\",\"find\":\"Prior to May 2025 we had four roles: `ReadOnly`, `User`, `PowerUser`, and `Admin`. We ran a developer survey and discovered that most users were defaulting to using the `PowerUser` role because `ReadOnly` didn't grant them access to everything they needed. Crucially, it did not grant them access to Amazon Athena. Developers found friction in using the`ReadOnly` role for most of their work, only to find they needed to switch roles to use Athena. In the end, most developers had begun to assume the `PowerUser` role by default, which granted them additional permissions in excess of that they _required_ to do their work.\",\"replace\":\"Prior to May 2025 we had four roles: `ReadOnly`, `User`, `PowerUser`, and `Admin`. A developer survey showed that most users defaulted to `PowerUser` because `ReadOnly` lacked access they needed, notably Amazon Athena. This gave them more permissions than they _required_.\"},{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i3\",\"find\":\"\",\"replace\":\"In response, we retired the `ReadOnly`, `User`, and `PowerUser` roles in favour of a new `Developer` role that better reflected developers' needs.\"},{\"op\":\"edit\",\"id\":\"s15.s2.i4\",\"find\":\"\",\"replace\":\"However, `Developer` can update and delete Kubernetes `Deployment` resources, which is useful but could take applications offline. As `Developer` had become the lowest level of privilege, this meant the lowest level could cause a production outage.\"}],\"flagged\":[]}",
